    grPlay:"Play it", grLvl:(n,k)=>"Level "+n+" · "+k+" chords", grLocked:n=>"Level "+n+" to play it", grClose:"Close the grid", grListH:"Mother grids", progBlues:"12-bar blues", grBlues:"Blues 12", grCanon:"Canon",
    grWhy:{g145:"Together, I, IV and V hold every note of the scale. The V builds tension, the I answers it: the music comes home.",
      pop:"The vi is the minor cousin of the I: they share two notes. The loop moves from bright to dark and back without ever stopping, which makes it so catchy.",
      pop2:"The same chords as I–V–vi–IV, but starting on the minor one: the loop sounds sadder, and the final V throws it back to the start.",
      ii5i:"Each chord falls a fifth onto the next (D → G → C in C). It is the most natural move in harmony, the backbone of jazz.",
      blues:"Three chords over 12 bars: four of I, two of IV, two of I, then V, IV, I and a V that starts it again. The form is so steady you can improvise over it.",
      fifties:"The I, then its relative minor vi, then IV and V lead back to I: a full turn in four chords, the grid of the 1950s ballads.",
      canon:"The bass walks down almost step by step while the chords follow each other: Pachelbel built a canon for three violins on it, and it has never aged.",
      m145:"The minor version of I–IV–V: same roles (rest, away, tension), darker colour. Many old tunes turn on these three chords."},
